<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Sylvester II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 946–1003</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Gerbert of Aurillac, Sylvester II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Sylvester II, born Gerbert of Aurillac, was a French scholar who became the first French pope, reigning from 999 to 1003. As a young monk he studied in Catalonia, at Vic and probably Ripoll, where he learned mathematics and astronomy drawn from Arabic sources, and he later taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. He promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu-Arabic numerals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and worked closely with Emperor Otto III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Sylvester II" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister der Reichenauer Schule · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Reichenauer_Schule_002_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerbert was born about 946, or at any rate between 945 and 950, in what was then the Duchy of Aquitaine. His exact birthplace is unknown. His parents took him to a nearby Benedictine abbey for instruction, and around 963 he entered the Monastery of St. Gerald of Aurillac. In 967 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona">Borrell II of Barcelona</a> took him to Catalonia to study mathematics. He studied under Bishop Atto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vic">Vich</a> and probably at Santa Maria de Ripoll, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> came from Muslim Spain, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. In 973 Archbishop Adalberon appointed him a teacher at the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gerbert learned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic digits and applied them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a>, probably without the numeral zero. His pupil Richer recorded that he could calculate with it far faster than others using Roman numerals. He used an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> to teach astronomy, built a hydraulic organ with brass pipes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>, and in 984 wrote to Abbot Eberhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> about founding a large scientific library. His writings on arithmetic, geometry and astronomy were mainly teaching texts for his students.</p><p>After Adalberon died in 989, King Hugh appointed Arnulf as archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>. Arnulf was deposed in 991 for alleged treason and Gerbert succeeded him, but a synod in 995 declared the deposition invalid. Gerbert then taught Otto III, and in 998 Pope Gregory V made him archbishop of Ravenna. With imperial support he was elected pope in 999 and took the name Sylvester II, after Sylvester I, the adviser of Emperor Constantine I. He confirmed Arnulf at Rheims and acted against simony and concubinage among the clergy. In 1001 the Roman populace revolted and forced Otto III and Sylvester to flee to Ravenna. Otto died in 1002 on a third expedition to regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sylvester returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> soon after the emperor's death, though the rebellious nobility remained in power. He died on 12 May 1003 and is buried in St. John Lateran. He was the first French pope. Later legends, found in William of Malmesbury and in a pamphlet by Cardinal Beno, a partisan of Emperor Henry IV, said that he stole a book of the dark arts from a Saracen philosopher, built a brazen head that answered yes or no, and made a pact with a demon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Sylvester II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gerbert of Aurillac</b>, the future Pope Sylvester II, studied in Catalonia in the 960s, where mathematics and astronomy from al-Andalus were beginning to circulate in Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was crowned king at Christmas 1000 or on 1 January 1001, with a crown that, according to his later legends, was sent by <b>Pope Sylvester II</b> with the approval of Emperor Otto III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Stephen's later legends his crown was sent by <b>Pope Sylvester II</b> with the approval of Emperor Otto III; the Holy Crown of Hungary that survives is traditionally linked with him, though its present form combines Byzantine enamel work of the later eleventh century with a Latin…</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>By traditional reckoning Grand Prince Stephen was crowned with a crown sent by <b>Pope Sylvester II</b>, making Hungary a Christian kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its church centres flourished: Ripoll's library made it a hub of learning in the 10th century, where <b>Gerbert of Aurillac</b> studied Arabic science.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-osona">County of Osona</a></li><li><blockquote>Géza's son Vajk, baptised Stephen, defeated the pagan Koppány and was crowned king on Christmas Day 1000 (or 1 January 1001) with a crown from <b>Pope Sylvester II</b>, founding the Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301).</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Lateran Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.51&amp;year=983" aria-label="Lateran Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pope Sylvester II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.51&amp;year=983">What was here in 983 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
